ues, including problems related to the adrenal glands.\n\nThe disorder is notable for its variable phenotypic expression, meaning that symptoms can differ significantly among affected individuals. It is relevant in the differential diagnosis of mitochondrial diseases due to its overlapping symptoms. Management of adrenoleucodistrofia often requires dietary interventions to help mitigate the effects of the disorder. Overall, adrenoleucodistrofia represents a complex interplay of genetic and metabolic factors that necessitate careful clinical attention.\",52\r\n10759,DRA. M.ª NURIA VIRGILI CASAS,Dra. M.ª Nuria Virgili Casas is a medical professional specializing in lipid disorders and their dietary management.,2\r\n8244,DIETARY RESTRICTIONS,\"Dietary restrictions are essential for managing the health of patients with adrenoleucodistrofia, primarily aimed at limiting the intake of very long-chain fatty acids. In addition to this specific restriction, dietary guidelines for these patients may also encompass a variety of other limitations, including gluten-free, low-purine, and vegetarian options. These comprehensive dietary restrictions are designed to support the overall well-being and nutritional needs of individuals affected by this condition.\",2\r\n11003,ADRENAL INSUFFICIENCY,\"Adrenal insufficiency is a common complication in patients with adrenoleucodistrofia, often requiring steroid treatment.\",1\r\n5042,GEN X-ALD,\"The GEN X-ALD gene is responsible for encoding a protein that functions as a peroxisomal membrane transporter, specifically involved in the transport of fatty acids within cells. Mutations in the X-ALD gene result in a condition known as adrenoleukodystrophy, which is characterized by the accumulation of very long-chain fatty acids due to impaired transport. This genetic alteration can lead to severe neurological and adrenal dysfunction, highlighting the critical role of the X-ALD gene in cellular fatty acid metabolism.\",1\r\n10763,DRA. ROSA BURGOS